WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.570
In addition to L2 regularization,

00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:06.730
another very powerful regularization technique is called dropout.

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:08.510
Let's see how that works.

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:12.410
Let's say you've trained a neural network like the one on the left and it's overfitting.

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:14.510
Here's what you do with dropout.

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:16.970
Let me make a copy of the neural network.

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:21.130
With dropout, what we're going to do is go through each of the layers of

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:26.330
the network and set some probability of eliminating a node in the neural network.

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:29.290
So let's say that for each of these layers,

00:00:29.290 --> 00:00:31.050
we're going to, for each node,

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:38.090
toss a coin and have a 0.5 chance of keeping each node and 0.5 chance of removing each node.

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:39.810
So after the coin tosses,

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:42.850
maybe we'll decide to eliminate those nodes.

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:46.330
Then what you do is actually remove

00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:49.570
all the outgoing links from that node as well.

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:51.550
So you end up with a much smaller,

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:53.250
really much diminished network.

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:55.610
Then you do back propagation,

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:59.410
training this one example on this much diminished network.

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:01.090
Then on different example,

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:04.610
you would toss a set of coins again and keep a different set of nodes,

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:07.410
and then dropout or eliminate different set of nodes.

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:09.250
So for each training example,

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:14.250
you would train it using one of these reduced networks.

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:17.290
So maybe it seems like a slightly crazy technique to just go

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:20.490
around killing nodes at random,

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:22.250
but this actually works.

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:25.130
But you can imagine that because you're training

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:28.690
a much smaller network on each example,

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:34.330
maybe just give a sense for why you end up able to regularize the network,

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:38.610
because these much smaller networks are being trained.

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:41.370
So let's look at how you implement dropout.

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:43.330
There are a few ways of implementing dropout.

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:44.930
I'm going to show you the most common one,

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:47.570
which is this technique called inverted dropout.

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:49.650
For the sake of completeness,

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:55.690
let's say we want to illustrate this with

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:59.610
layer L equals 3.

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:01.250
So in the code I'm going to write,

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:03.210
there'll be a bunch of 3s here.

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:08.210
That's just, I'm just going to illustrate how to implement dropout in a single layer.

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:12.530
So what we're going to do is set a vector D,

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:16.490
and D3 is going to be the dropout vector for layer 3.

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:17.810
That's what the 3 is,

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:22.890
to be np.random.rand.

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:28.170
This is going to be the same shape as A3.

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:31.090
I'm going to see if this is less than some number,

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:34.410
which I'm going to call keepprop.

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:37.130
So keepprop is a number.

00:02:37.130 --> 00:02:39.130
It was 0.5 on the previous slide,

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:42.090
but maybe now I'll use 0.8 in this example.

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:46.890
It'll be the probability that a given hidden unit will be kept.

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:49.330
So if keepprop is equal to 0.8,

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:54.570
then this means that there's a 0.2 chance of eliminating any hidden unit.

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:58.050
So what this does is it generates a random matrix,

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:00.770
and this works as well if you have vectorized.

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:06.090
So D3 will be a matrix where for each example and for each hidden unit,

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:10.250
there's a 0.8 chance that the corresponding D3 will be one,

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:12.770
and the 20 percent chance there'll be zero.

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:17.650
So this random number being less than 0.8,

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:20.890
that's a 0.8 chance of being one or being true,

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:24.570
and a 20 percent chance of 0.2 chance of being false or being zero.

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:27.530
Then what you're going to do is take your activations from the third layer,

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:30.810
and we'll just call that A3 in this little example.

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:33.170
So A3 are the activations you computed,

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:39.170
and you're going to set A3 to be equal to the old A3 times.

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:41.770
So this is element-wise multiplication.

00:03:41.770 --> 00:03:46.290
Well, I guess you could also write this as A3 times equals D3.

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:50.490
But what this does is for every element of D3 that's equal to zero,

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:53.730
and there was a 20 percent chance of each of the elements being zero,

00:03:53.730 --> 00:04:00.730
you end up, this multiply operation ends up zeroing out the corresponding element of D3.

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:02.250
If you do this in Python,

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:06.970
technically D3 will be a Boolean array with values true and false rather than one and zero,

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:08.290
but you'll multiply,

00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:13.410
the multiply operation works and interpret the true and false values as one and zero.

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:16.210
If you try this yourself in Python, you'll see.

00:04:16.210 --> 00:04:23.810
Then finally, we're going to take A3 and scale it up by dividing by 0.8,

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:29.810
or really dividing by our heap parameter.

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:32.530
So let me explain what this final step is doing.

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:36.130
Let's say for the sake of argument that you have 50 units or

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:39.810
50 neurons in the third hidden layer.

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:42.850
So maybe A3 is 50 by one dimensional,

00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:46.450
or if your factorization maybe is 50 by M dimensional.

00:04:46.450 --> 00:04:49.730
So if you have a 80 percent chance of keeping them,

00:04:49.730 --> 00:04:51.650
20 percent chance of eliminating them,

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:59.050
this means that on average you end up with 10 units shut off or 10 units zeroed out.

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:02.050
Now, if you look at the value of Z4,

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:08.930
Z4 is going to be equal to W4 times A3 plus B4.

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:14.050
And so on expectation, this will be reduced by 20 percent,

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:18.450
by which I mean that 20 percent of the elements of A3 will be zeroed out.

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:22.290
So in order to not reduce the expected value of Z4,

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:28.450
what you do is you need to take this and divide it by 0.8,

00:05:28.450 --> 00:05:33.650
because this will, you know, correct or this will bump it back up by the roughly 20 percent that you need.

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:37.410
So it's to not change the expected value of A3.

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:43.330
And so this line here is what's called the inverted dropout technique.

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:47.090
And its effect is that no matter what you set the key prop to,

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:50.130
whether it's 0.8 or 0.9 or even 1,

00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:54.610
if you set it to 1 then there's no dropout because you're keeping everything or 0.5 or whatever.

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:58.130
This inverted dropout technique, by dividing by the key prop,

00:05:58.130 --> 00:06:02.610
it ensures that the expected value of A3 remains the same.

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:06.690
And it turns out that at test time when you're trying to evaluate the neural network,

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:09.970
which talk about on the next slide, this inverted dropout technique,

00:06:09.970 --> 00:06:13.090
there's this line that I drew the green box around,

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:17.730
this makes test time easier because you have less of a scaling problem.

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:21.090
But by far the most common implementation of dropout today,

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:22.770
far as I know, is inverted dropouts.

00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:24.450
I recommend you just implement this.

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:30.130
But there were some early iterations of dropout that missed this divide by key prop line.

00:06:30.130 --> 00:06:33.490
And so at test time, the algorithm became a little bit more complicated.

00:06:33.490 --> 00:06:36.610
But again, people tend not to use those other versions.

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:40.130
So what you do is you use the D vector.

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:46.050
And you notice that for different training examples, you zero out different hidden units.

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:49.970
And in fact, if you make multiple passes through the same training set,

00:06:49.970 --> 00:06:52.610
then on different passes through the training set,

00:06:52.610 --> 00:06:55.250
you should randomly zero out different hidden units.

00:06:55.250 --> 00:07:00.210
So it's not that for one example, you should keep zeroing out the different same hidden units.

00:07:00.210 --> 00:07:05.330
Is that on iteration one of gradient descent, you might zero out some hidden units.

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:07.410
And on the second iteration of gradient descent,

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:09.570
where you go through the training set a second time,

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:12.930
maybe you set zero out a different pattern of hidden units.

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:18.450
And the vector D or D3 for the third layer is used to decide what to zero out,

00:07:18.450 --> 00:07:21.330
both in forward prop as well as in back prop.

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:22.850
We're just showing forward prop here.

00:07:22.850 --> 00:07:26.930
Now having trained the algorithm, at test time, here's what you would do.

00:07:26.930 --> 00:07:30.530
At test time, you're given some x on which you want to make a prediction.

00:07:30.530 --> 00:07:34.050
And using our standard notation, I'm going to use A0,

00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:38.130
the activations of the zero layer to denote this test example x.

00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:42.290
So what we're going to do is not use dropout at test time.

00:07:42.290 --> 00:07:48.690
In particular, we're just going to set Z1 equals W1 A0 plus B1.

00:07:48.690 --> 00:08:05.490
A1 equals G1 of Z1, Z2 equals W2 A1 plus B2, A2 equals, and so on,

00:08:05.490 --> 00:08:09.810
until you get to the last layer and then you make a prediction y hat.

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:13.650
But notice that at test time, you're not using dropout explicitly,

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:15.650
in that you're not tossing coins at random.

00:08:15.650 --> 00:08:19.330
You're not flipping coins to decide which hidden unit is to eliminate.

00:08:20.210 --> 00:08:22.370
And that's because when you're making predictions at test time,

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:24.770
you don't really want your output to be random.

00:08:25.490 --> 00:08:29.810
If you were implementing dropout at test time, that just adds noise to your predictions.

00:08:29.810 --> 00:08:35.410
In theory, one thing you could do is run a prediction process many times with different

00:08:35.410 --> 00:08:38.850
hidden units randomly dropped out and then averaged across them.

00:08:38.850 --> 00:08:43.490
But that's computationally inefficient and it gives you roughly the same result,

00:08:43.490 --> 00:08:46.770
very, very similar result to this particular procedure as well.

00:08:46.770 --> 00:08:49.250
And just to mention the inverted dropout thing,

00:08:49.250 --> 00:08:53.410
remember the step on the previous slide where we divided by the keep prop.

00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:57.170
The effect of that was ensured that even when you don't implement dropout

00:08:57.970 --> 00:09:01.970
at test time to the scaling, the expected value of these activations don't change.

00:09:01.970 --> 00:09:05.730
So you don't need to add in an extra funny scaling parameter

00:09:05.730 --> 00:09:08.290
at test time that's different than when you had at training time.

00:09:08.850 --> 00:09:10.130
So that's dropout.

00:09:10.130 --> 00:09:12.930
And when you implement this in this week's primary exercise,

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:15.890
you gain more firsthand experience with it as well.

00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:18.290
But why does it really work?

00:09:18.290 --> 00:09:21.490
What I want to do in the next video is give you some better intuition

00:09:21.490 --> 00:09:23.570
about what dropout really is doing.

00:09:23.570 --> 00:09:25.170
Let's go on to the next video.
